 "text": "Hello there! How's your day treating you? Think of it as two parts. The first is a case ending, like dative or genitive. The second is the noun itself, and its case is usually marked by an ending. So, for example, man becomes man in the dative, and man becomes mein in the genitive. It does at first, but it gets lot easier with practice. What tends to trip you up most? Very common. The trick is looking at the word and thinking about the ending first, rather than the noun itself. It' a bit like that. Each case gets coloured differently, so learning the colours helps the connections.",
